# Implementation

## 1. Dedicated Splunk Network Index

A dedicated Splunk index was created for network-security telemetry:

```text
network
```

Separating network telemetry from Windows endpoint logs provides cleaner data organization and simplifies firewall-focused searches.

Example:

```spl
index=network
```

---

## 2. Splunk Syslog Input

Splunk Enterprise was configured to receive pfSense syslog traffic over:

```text
Protocol: UDP
Port:     5514
Index:    network
```

The resulting telemetry path is:

```text
FW01
10.10.10.1
     |
     | UDP/5514
     v
SIEM01
10.10.10.20
     |
     v
Splunk
index=network
```

The UDP listener was validated on SIEM01 before configuring the remote sender.

---

## 3. SIEM Host Firewall Configuration

Windows Defender Firewall on SIEM01 was configured to permit the syslog traffic.

Rather than allowing UDP/5514 from the entire network, the rule was restricted to FW01.

```powershell
New-NetFirewallRule `
  -DisplayName "LAB - Allow pfSense Syslog" `
  -Direction Inbound `
  -Protocol UDP `
  -LocalPort 5514 `
  -RemoteAddress 10.10.10.1 `
  -Action Allow `
  -Profile Any
```

This permits:

```text
10.10.10.1 -> SIEM01:5514/UDP
```

without exposing the Splunk syslog listener unnecessarily to other hosts.

---

## 4. pfSense Remote Logging

FW01 was configured to forward security telemetry to:

```text
10.10.10.20:5514
```

The LAN interface was used as the source for remote logging.

Selected log categories included:

- Firewall Events
- System Events

This provided firewall-security telemetry without forwarding every available pfSense log category.

